@database "message_validator"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: message_validator.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Message Validator"
@{h1}Message Validator

Providers refuse a whole request when the conversation history breaks
their rules: a tool call nobody answered, a tool answer to a call that is
not there, a message wedged between a tool call and its answer, a tool
name with a dot in it. Such histories happen -- a run is cancelled while
its tools work, a blocked call gets no answer, a summary cuts the start
of a conversation. Message Validator looks at the history before every
LLM call and repairs its @{i}structure@{ui} so the request goes through.
It never rewrites what a message says, except that two assistant turns in
a row are joined into one, and a turn that loses all its tool calls and has
no text reads "Tool execution was interrupted". It has no tools and no
panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The validation hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What it repairs  " link repairs}
  @{"  What it only reports  " link reports}
  @{"  The structure hook  " link structure}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node hook "The validation hook"
@{h1}The validation hook

@{tt}validate_messages@{utt} is a @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} hook: it runs
before every LLM call of every agent -- the web chat, sub-agents, woken
runs, the one extra call after @{tt}max_steps@{utt} -- as long as the
plugin is enabled and the agent has not switched hooks off. It is on by
default. It runs after the hooks of the @{tt}prompt_injection@{utt}
category and before message_debugger's.

@{h2}What it does

@{bullet}It checks the whole history the call is about to send (the list
of messages, before the LLM client turns it into a provider's request) --
see @{" What it repairs " link repairs} and
@{" What it only reports " link reports}.
@{bullet}Nothing found, or only things it does not repair: the history is
left exactly as it was.
@{bullet}Something repaired: the repaired list is what the call sends, and
it replaces the session's history, so the repair happens once and is
saved with the session. Every repair also resets the provider's reasoning
chain (the encrypted or signed reasoning an assistant turn carries) from
the first changed message on: the assistant turns there lose theirs, and
the newest is marked as having lost its predecessors -- a chain the history
no longer matches would be refused. Turns before the first change keep
theirs.
@{bullet}It adds no message of its own, so it sets no @{tt}injected_by@{utt}
mark. Messages it moves or joins keep theirs.

The hook returns two figures in the hook metadata, and the log carries
the rest:

@{table}
Metadata key | Meaning
validation_result | "No issues found", or e.g. "Found 1 errors, 2 warnings: 1 orphaned_tool_call, 2 consecutive_assistant_messages"
issues_count | how many issues it found, repaired or not
@{body}

In the log: @{i}Message validation found 3 issues in
message_validator: ...@{ui} whenever it found anything, @{i}Hook 'message_validator.validate_messages'
repaired 3 issues@{ui} when it changed the history, and one line per
moved message and per unanswered tool call.

@{h2}Switching it off

For one agent, in its YAML:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      message_validator.validate_messages:
        enabled: false
@{body}

The key is the full name, @{tt}<instance>.<hook>@{utt}. The hook has no
per-agent settings; anything else in the override is ignored. Switching
it off is rarely right: a provider then refuses the requests the hook
would have repaired, and the agent loop counts on it to drop a tool call
that loop detection blocked.
@endnode

@node repairs "What it repairs"
@{h1}What it repairs

Each check below names the issue type that appears in the log and in
@{tt}validation_result@{utt}. Tool calls and answers are paired per
@{i}block@{ui}: an assistant message with tool calls opens one, and the
next assistant message with tool calls closes it. Only answers inside the
open block count for its calls -- providers want the answers right after
their calls, and a call id may come back in a later turn.

@{table}
Issue | Found when | Repair
orphaned_tool_call | a tool call gets no answer in its block (or has no id) | that call is taken out of the message; answered calls of the same message stay. A turn left with no call and no text -- also after joining -- reads "Tool execution was interrupted"
orphaned_tool_response | a tool answer's @{tt}tool_call_id@{utt} matches no unanswered call of the open block -- also a late answer after the next call turn | the tool answer is removed
missing_tool_call_id | a tool answer has no @{tt}tool_call_id@{utt} | the tool answer is removed
invalid_tool_name | a called tool's name is not letters, digits, _ and - only | the name is rewritten; if nothing usable is left, that call and its answer are removed, the message and its other calls stay
empty_assistant_message | an assistant message has no tool call and no text (none, empty or blank) | the message is removed
consecutive_assistant_messages | two assistant messages follow each other | they are joined into the first (see below)
interleaved_message_in_tool_block | a non-tool message stands between a tool call and the answers still owed to it | it is moved to after the tool answers that follow it
invalid_first_message | the first message after the system and developer instructions is not a user turn (or the task of a woken run) | removed, together with the tool answers of its calls, until a user turn comes first
@{body}

@{h2}Tool names

The rewrite: @{tt}/@{utt} becomes @{tt}__@{utt}, @{tt}.@{utt} and spaces
become @{tt}_@{utt}, every other character outside letters, digits,
@{tt}_@{utt} and @{tt}-@{utt} is dropped, three or more underscores
shrink to two, and underscores at both ends go.

@{table}
Called as | Sent as
file_ops.read | file_ops_read
server/tool | server__tool
my tool! | my_tool
@{body}

@{h2}Joining assistant turns

The text of both is joined with a blank line, and so is their reasoning
text; their tool calls, attachments and reasoning details are put
together in order. Anthropic thinking blocks are not concatenated: the
joined message keeps the later turn's blocks, or the earlier one's when
the later has none. Name, timestamp and @{tt}injected_by@{utt} are the
first message's. A run of three or more
becomes one message. An empty first turn is not lost in the join: the
joined message takes its place.

@{h2}In what order

The repairs are made in this order: tool names renamed, tool calls
without answers (and calls with unusable names) dropped, moved messages, joined turns, removals, the placeholder text, then the
first-message rule. A repair can expose another issue -- two assistant
turns meet once a message between them is removed -- so check and repair
run again until nothing changes, at most three times per call. After
that, every call turn is followed directly by exactly its answers, no
tool answer stands alone, no two assistant turns meet, and a user turn
comes first.
@endnode

@node reports "What it only reports"
@{h1}What it only reports

These checks log a warning and count in @{tt}issues_count@{utt}, but
change nothing:

@{table}
Issue | Found when
invalid_tool_response_json | a tool answer begins with @{tt}{@{utt} or @{tt}[@{utt} but is not valid JSON
tool_response_too_large | a tool answer is larger than @{tt}max_tool_response_size_kb@{utt} (UTF-8 bytes)
tool_response_large | a tool answer is larger than @{tt}warn_tool_response_size_kb@{utt}, but not than the maximum
@{body}

A tool answer that is plain text, or valid JSON of any kind, is fine. The
JSON check fires on text that only looks like JSON as well, such as a
tool answer starting with "[1/3] fetched". Tool answers without text are
not checked.

The size figures only warn: a tool answer over the maximum is still sent
in full. To make tool answers smaller, cap them where they are made or
with the context plugins. Such a tool answer stays in the history, so its
warning is logged again before every call.
@endnode

@node structure "The structure hook"
@{h1}The structure hook

@{tt}validate_structure@{utt} runs as a @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} hook
right before @{tt}validate_messages@{utt}, on by default like it. It
looks for messages whose role is missing or empty, logs @{i}Structure
validation issues: Message 3 missing role@{ui} and reports the list as
@{tt}structure_errors@{utt} in the hook metadata. It never changes the
history. Switch it off with
@{tt}message_validator.validate_structure: {enabled: false}@{utt} in the
agent's hook overrides.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

Nothing of the plugin itself: no tools, no instructions, no status line in
the chat. The model sees the repaired history -- a tool call that was never
answered is gone from its turn, a turn whose calls were all dropped and
that has no text reads "Tool execution was interrupted", a message that had slipped between a
tool call and its answer comes after the answer.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

@{bullet}A clean history is left alone -- nothing is rewritten, so every
request stays the start of the next and the provider's prompt cache holds.
Issues it only reports change nothing either.
@{bullet}A repair rewrites the history from the first repaired message on,
and so does the reasoning reset; everything before it stays as it was. The
next request misses the cache from that message on. Because the repaired
history replaces the session's, this happens once per defect, not on every
call.
@{bullet}The hook sees the plugin's own message list, before an LLM client
turns it into a provider's request (a chat @{tt}messages@{utt} list or a
Responses @{tt}input@{utt} list); it works the same for every provider. The
tool-name rule is OpenAI's and is applied everywhere.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A tool answer that comes later in its block, after other
messages, counts as answered; the messages in between are moved, not
removed.
@{bullet}The settings are read when the plugin is created; a change takes
effect after a restart.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration
(@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}), and its hooks run for every agent:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    message_validator:
      type: message_validator
      enabled: true
      config:
        max_tool_response_size_kb: 9999
        warn_tool_response_size_kb: 9998
@{body}

No tool, so no allowlist line. No extra packages.

@{h2}Server settings

These go under @{tt}config:@{utt}; what is not set there takes the default
below. The values are not checked; the size figures of the shipped entry
switch the size warnings off in practice.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_tool_response_size_kb | 50 | a tool answer above this many KB is logged as tool_response_too_large
warn_tool_response_size_kb | 20 | a tool answer above this many KB is logged as tool_response_large
@{body}

The plugin stores nothing.
@endnode
